    "body": "Bisect and analysis (issue-worker-high):\n\n**Culprit: PR #1306 (merge `e74bca1f9`, issue #1303 tilt-band dip).** Release lane, both swarf full-band tests: v0.18.0 green; `a0d102834` (main just before #1306) green; `e74bca1f9` red with the same 18 hits; every later probe (before #1324, before #1319, current main) red.\n\n**Which side is wrong: the check.** Every hit is a swarf link move whose B flips +90 -> -90 (or back) with A = C = 0, the blade-lying axis turning through vertical. The cells convicted columns about 47 mm from the tip, where the band is really ~40 mm above the tip at that pose (B ~ 45), with a cell bottom of `tip - 3` mm.\n\nThat bottom comes from #1303's span floor (`tilt_span::span_floor`), read at the sampled pose B = 45 against its neighbour B = 90. `in_cap` asks for every A/B letter *strictly* inside (-90, 90), so an end at exactly 90 drops the span to the interval bound: cos range [0, 0.71], `sin_max = 1`, floor = `-r`. The cell holding frac 0.25 is pinned to that floor, but over B = 45's footprint. The real dip (the horizontal band, `tip - r`) is at the B = 90 end itself, which its own cells already read in its own place.\n\nThe module's own law covers a closed cap. With every letter in [-90, 90], cos A and cos B stay >= 0 along the lerp, and `ln cos A + ln cos B` is concave there. So `cos θ` takes its minimum at an end, by continuity also when that end is exactly 90. The more tilted sample already bounds the span. Only letters *past* 90 (the #1303 cases: A 100 -> 260, 60 -> 120) can dip below both samples, and they keep the interval floor.\n\nFix under way: close the cap (`<= 90`), pinned by a brute-force span test with ends at exactly +-90 in the default lane. Both heavy tests are green in release with it.\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
